window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][32] = "<h1>Part VI — Strategy</h1>\n<span id=\"c18\"></span>\n<h2>C18 — Strategy at APM Depth</h2>\n<span id=\"c18-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;Should a device maker launch a subscription bundle of its services?&quot; &quot;A competitor just launched a free tier. How do you respond?&quot; &quot;Should we build, buy or partner for this capability?&quot; &quot;How would you decide whether to enter a new market?&quot; These are strategy questions, and at the associate level they are not asking for a vision of the industry. They are asking whether you can reason like an owner: diagnose the situation, lay out real options, weigh them against criteria with numbers, and commit to a recommendation with its risks. Candidates fail them in two opposite ways: by reciting frameworks (SWOT, five forces) without a point of view, and by giving an opinion with no structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structures candidates commonly report: strategy and business-judgment questions at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Netflix, Stripe and others, often as a case of 20 to 30 minutes, with market-entry, competitive-response, build-or-partner and pricing or bundling variants <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. On the job, APMs are rarely asked to set company strategy; they are asked to understand it, to connect their product's choices to it, and to contribute a clear analysis when a strategic question arises.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Whether your reasoning is structured, quantitative and committed <code>Inferred</code>. Interviewers listen for a diagnosis before options; options that differ in kind; criteria tied to the objective; unit economics and a market size with reality checks; an understanding of what makes an advantage durable; second-order effects (cannibalization, competitor response, regulation); a recommendation with a sequence; and a statement of what would change your mind.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You clarify the objective and the company's situation, and diagnose before proposing.</li>\n<li>You size the opportunity with two routes and a reality check.</li>\n<li>You analyze competition by customer value and positioning, not by feature tables.</li>\n<li>You name the source of advantage and test whether it is durable.</li>\n<li>You ground the recommendation in unit economics and sensitivities, and name the second-order effects.</li>\n<li>You commit to a recommendation, sequence it, and say what would change your mind.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end strategy answers: a framework recited with no conclusion, a market size with no reality check, a feature comparison taken for competitive analysis, &quot;network effects&quot; named without a mechanism, ignoring cannibalization and competitor response, and a roadmap presented as a strategy.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c18-c18-1-what-strategy-is-diagnosis-guiding-policy-coherent-act\"></span>\n<h4>C18.1 What strategy is: diagnosis, guiding policy, coherent action</h4>\n<p><strong>Strategy</strong> is a coherent set of choices about where to compete and how to win, grounded in a diagnosis of the situation. A useful way to see the structure is the &quot;kernel&quot; from Richard Rumelt (FW-C18-01): a <em>diagnosis</em> of the challenge (what is going on, and what is the crux?), a <em>guiding policy</em> (the approach chosen to deal with it, which includes what you will not do), and <em>coherent actions</em> (steps that reinforce one another and carry out the policy).</p>\n<p><strong>Why choices are the point.</strong> A strategy that says yes to everything is a wish list. Strategy is as much about what you decline as what you pursue, because resources and attention are limited (C15's opportunity cost). A statement such as &quot;be the best and grow 30% a year&quot; is a goal. A strategy says: &quot;We will win small clinics, not hospitals, because we can serve them better and they are underserved; so we will build for a front desk of two people, sell by referral and not by sales team, and decline custom integrations.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>At APM scale.</strong> Most APM-level strategy questions are about a product, a market or a decision, not an entire company. The same kernel applies: what is the situation, what approach do you take, and what do you do first.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use a full strategy analysis.</strong> For a tactical question (which feature first) use C15; for a question about why a metric moved, use C13. Strategy is for choices that are expensive to reverse and shape many later choices (entering a market, building versus buying, a pricing model).</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> Asked how a company should respond to a rival's price cut, a candidate says: &quot;I'd do a SWOT. Strengths: brand. Weaknesses: price. Opportunities: new markets. Threats: the rival. Then I'd consider the five forces.&quot; There is no diagnosis (why did the rival cut, and whom does it hurt?), no options, no numbers and no recommendation. A better start: &quot;First, who is at risk? If the cut targets our price-sensitive 30% of customers, the question is whether to defend them, and at what cost, which I can size.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risks.</strong> Two. <em>Frameworks as substitutes for thinking:</em> SWOT and five forces are lists; they help organize a diagnosis and cannot replace one. <em>Hindsight:</em> it is easy to explain a winner's strategy after the fact; analyze with the information available at the time and say what you do not know.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-c18-2-vision-mission-and-product-principles\"></span>\n<h4>C18.2 Vision, mission and product principles</h4>\n<p><strong>Vision</strong> is the future a company or product aims to bring about; <strong>mission</strong> is what it does and for whom; <strong>product principles</strong> are the choices that guide tradeoffs when goals conflict (for example, &quot;simple over powerful,&quot; &quot;private by default,&quot; &quot;fast over feature-rich&quot;). In interviews you should not recite a company's mission. Use it as a test: does the option serve the stated purpose, and does it fit the principles the product has chosen?</p>\n<p>Principles do real work when they settle arguments. A team with the principle &quot;a first-time user should succeed without help&quot; has an answer when asked to add an advanced option to the first screen. A principle that cannot say no to anything is a slogan. Companies publish missions and sometimes principles <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Google's &quot;Ten things we know to be true&quot; (archived copy); Amazon's Leadership Principles page; Netflix's culture page; read October 2026]</code>; use public statements as evidence of intent and say that you are inferring how they apply.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Principles turn strategy into daily decisions. <strong>When not to dwell.</strong> A mission line takes a sentence in an answer; the analysis is the substance.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-c18-3-market-sizing-with-reality-checks\"></span>\n<h4>C18.3 Market sizing with reality checks</h4>\n<p>A market-size estimate tells you whether an opportunity could be big enough to matter, and it is only useful if you test it. The <strong>total addressable market</strong> (TAM) is the total revenue available if every potential customer bought the product. Narrower layers are more useful: the <em>serviceable</em> market (what you can reach with your product and channels) and the <em>obtainable</em> share (what you could win in a given period). FW-C18-03 gives the method.</p>\n<p><strong>Two routes.</strong> <em>Top-down</em> starts from a large population and applies filters: people, then those who fit, then adoption, then price. <em>Bottom-up</em> starts from units you can count: customers or sites you could reach, times how many would buy, times what they pay. Compare them. If they differ by a factor of two or three, find the assumption that explains the gap.</p>\n<p><strong>Reality checks.</strong> Compare with known quantities: the revenue of existing providers, what customers spend today on the nearest alternative, the number of transactions the market could physically sustain, and adoption rates seen for similar products. A claim that a market is &quot;$50 billion&quot; because it is 1% of a $5 trillion industry is not a size; it is a slogan, and a favorite error in business plans.</p>\n<p><strong>Example (invented).</strong> Corporate lunch catering in a mid-sized city. <em>Top-down:</em> 315,000 office workers; suppose 15% order group lunches, 4 times a month at $12 a meal: 315,000 × 0.15 × 48 meals a year × $12 = $27.2 million a year. <em>Bottom-up:</em> 6,000 offices with 20 or more staff; 25% order group lunches; 2 orders a month at $350: 6,000 × 0.25 × 24 × $350 = $12.6 million a year. The top-down figure is 2.2 times the bottom-up. Why? The top-down approach counts 15% of all workers regardless of whether their office orders; the bottom-up requires an office-level decision and a lower frequency. The honest statement is a range: $12 to $27 million, with a planning number near the lower end for a new entrant, to be tested with a pilot. And then the question that matters: what share could we obtain, and is it enough? At a 10% share, the lower figure is $1.3 million a year, which may not cover a team.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Two routes and a reality check protect against the false precision of a single estimate. <strong>When not to size at length.</strong> If the decision does not depend on whether the market is $20 million or $200 million, say so and move on (C04's so-what bridge).</p>\n<span id=\"c18-c18-4-competitive-analysis-beyond-feature-tables-positioning\"></span>\n<h4>C18.4 Competitive analysis beyond feature tables; positioning</h4>\n<p>A feature table compares what products have; it says nothing about why customers choose. Better analysis starts with the customer's alternatives (including doing nothing, a spreadsheet or a substitute outside the category, C06) and what they value.</p>\n<p><strong>Positioning</strong> is the place a product occupies in customers' minds relative to the alternatives. A positioning statement has a target customer, the need or job, the category, the key benefit and the reason to believe. It is a choice: a product cannot be best at everything.</p>\n<p><strong>A positioning map (FW-C18-04).</strong> Choose two axes that matter to customers (not to the company): for example, speed of setup against depth of capability, or price against service level. Place the alternatives. Look for crowded areas (hard to win), empty areas (a gap, or a place with no demand), and where your product's strength lies. Ask for each competitor: what is their position, what are they good at, what can they not do without hurting their own business (the position a rival cannot copy because it would cannibalize them), and how will they respond?</p>\n<p><strong>A named alternative: Blue Ocean.</strong> Kim and Mauborgne's <em>Blue Ocean Strategy</em> (2005, from a 2004 <em>Harvard Business Review</em> article) asks you to stop competing on the factors the industry already competes on and to look for &quot;value innovation&quot;: raising buyer value and lowering cost at the same time. Its tools are a strategy canvas (plot each competitor's level on the factors buyers weigh) and four questions about each factor: which to eliminate, reduce, raise or create <code>[V: Kim and Mauborgne, Blue Ocean Strategy, Harvard Business Review Press, 2005; the authors' framework as summarized by Strategy &amp; Leadership and strategy teaching notes]</code>. Use it as a prompt for finding a gap on the positioning map, and treat any claimed gap as a hypothesis to test with customers, not as proof that demand exists <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Anticipate response.</strong> A move that threatens a strong competitor invites a reply. Consider what they can do quickly (cut price, copy a feature, bundle) and what they cannot (change their cost structure, abandon a core customer).</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Customer-value positioning exposes the real basis of competition. <strong>When not to rely on it.</strong> Axes chosen after the fact to flatter a favorite are decoration; choose them from customer research or ask what customers value.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-c18-5-sources-of-advantage-distribution-data-switching-costs\"></span>\n<h4>C18.5 Sources of advantage: distribution, data, switching costs, network effects, ecosystem integration, scale, brand</h4>\n<p>A <strong>moat</strong> is a durable advantage that protects a business from competitors. Strategic analysis asks where an advantage comes from and whether it lasts. FW-C18-05 tests each source.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Durable when</th>\n<th>Weak when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Distribution</td>\n<td>Access to customers that rivals lack (pre-installation, a sales network, an existing user base)</td>\n<td>It is hard or costly to replicate and customers do not mind</td>\n<td>Rivals can buy the same channel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data</td>\n<td>Information that improves the product and that rivals cannot get</td>\n<td>More data keeps improving the product and access is exclusive</td>\n<td>The advantage saturates, or data can be bought or is regulated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Switching costs</td>\n<td>A <strong>switching cost</strong> is the cost, in money, time, effort or risk, a customer bears to change provider</td>\n<td>Customers have built habits, data or integrations on you</td>\n<td>Export and import are easy, or rivals pay the cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Network effects</td>\n<td>A <strong>network effect</strong> is a property in which each additional user increases the product's value to the other users</td>\n<td>Value truly rises with users and users cannot easily coordinate to move</td>\n<td>The network is local or thin, or multi-homing is easy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ecosystem integration</td>\n<td>Products working together better than apart (hardware, software, services)</td>\n<td>Users are invested in several parts and parts reinforce each other</td>\n<td>Integration is closed in a way users resent, or standards open it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scale</td>\n<td>Lower cost per unit with volume, or learning from volume</td>\n<td>Fixed costs are large and the leader can pass savings on</td>\n<td>Costs do not fall with volume, or smaller rivals can access the same inputs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Brand</td>\n<td>Trust and preference</td>\n<td>Built over time on real performance</td>\n<td>Easily imitated or damaged</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Test each claimed advantage.</strong> Does the mechanism exist, or is the word being used loosely (&quot;network effects&quot; for any product with users)? Is it durable, and what would erode it? Does it compound (the leader gets stronger)? Can a rival copy it, and at what cost? Network effects are often misapplied: a messaging app has one where users want to reach friends there; a document editor's collaboration feature has a weaker one; a product with many users but no interaction between them has none.</p>\n<p><strong>Ecosystem integration</strong> is the one most relevant to device makers and platform owners, and it ties to C19: a customer who owns a phone, a watch and earbuds from one maker gains from each working with the others, and is less likely to leave.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Naming the mechanism and testing durability separates real moats from slogans. <strong>When not to claim one.</strong> Early products rarely have a moat; the honest answer is that speed and learning are the advantage, and what you would build toward.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-c18-6-business-model-and-unit-economics-at-apm-depth\"></span>\n<h4>C18.6 Business model and unit economics at APM depth</h4>\n<p>A business model answers who pays, for what, how, and at what cost. <strong>Unit economics</strong> are the revenue and cost attached to a single unit, such as a customer or an order, showing whether each unit makes money. <strong>Contribution margin</strong> is revenue minus the variable costs of a unit (cost of goods, delivery, payment fees, support), before fixed costs. C09.5 gave the basics for low revenue per user; here the aim is APM-level fluency (FW-C18-06).</p>\n<p><strong>The chain.</strong> Revenue per unit minus variable cost gives contribution per unit. Multiply by frequency for contribution per customer per month. The cost to acquire a customer (CAC) divided by monthly contribution gives the payback period. With churn, the expected lifetime is one over monthly churn, and lifetime contribution is monthly contribution times lifetime. Compare with CAC.</p>\n<p><strong>Example (invented).</strong> A marketplace entering grocery delivery: an average order of $80, a 22% gross margin ($17.60), picking and delivery cost $12 per order: contribution $5.60. Customers order 3 times a month ($16.80). Monthly churn 8%: lifetime 12.5 months: lifetime contribution $210. With CAC $40, the ratio is 5.3. Now let the average order be $60: gross margin $13.20, minus $12 is $1.20 per order, lifetime $45, ratio 1.1. A 25% smaller basket turns an attractive business into a marginal one, because the delivery cost is fixed per order. The strategic questions follow: how to raise basket size, lower delivery cost with density, and which neighborhoods to enter first.</p>\n<p><strong>Sensitivities and cautions.</strong> Name the two or three assumptions that swing the result (basket size, churn, delivery cost). Lifetime value forecasts are unreliable early because churn is uncertain; treat them as ranges. Include second-order effects: <strong>cannibalization</strong> (C08), price effects on the rest of the business, and cost allocation (shared costs are not variable costs). Pricing models (subscription, usage-based, transaction fee, advertising, freemium) each shift who bears risk and what behavior they encourage.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> A few lines of arithmetic show whether a strategy can make money and where it is fragile. <strong>When not to model deeply.</strong> In an interview, a clear chain with two sensitivities beats a spreadsheet.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-c18-7-answer-architectures-enter-a-market-respond-to-a-compe\"></span>\n<h4>C18.7 Answer architectures: enter a market, respond to a competitor, build or buy or partner, prioritize a business line</h4>\n<p>Four recurring forms. Section 4 gives the common spine, and the specifics:</p>\n<p><strong>Enter a market.</strong> Questions: Is the market attractive (size, growth, profit pool)? Do we have a right to win (an advantage there: customers, assets, capabilities)? Can we make money (unit economics, investment, timing)? What are the risks (competitors' response, regulation, execution)? Then how: build, buy or partner, which segment first, and what would make us stop. Criteria: attractiveness × right to win × economics × risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Respond to a competitor.</strong> Questions: What did they do and who does it hurt? Why did they do it (their position and incentives)? Options: ignore, match, differentiate, flank (serve a segment they cannot), or retaliate. Evaluate each by cost, effect on our economics and likely reply. Often the right response is narrower than the move: defend the segment at risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Build, buy or partner (FW-C18-07).</strong> Criteria: time to market, cost, control, strategic importance (is it core to our advantage?), capability fit, and risk (execution, integration, dependency). Build when it is core and you have the capability and time; buy when you need speed or a capability you lack and can integrate it; partner when it is non-core, speed matters and the dependency is acceptable. Compare over the relevant horizon (Worked example 3).</p>\n<p><strong>Prioritize a business line.</strong> Compare lines by size, growth, margin, strategic fit and investment needed; separate lines that fund the company (mature, profitable) from those that grow it and those that are experiments. Allocate capital to the highest return on strategic goals while protecting the cash source.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Forms speed up structure so thinking goes to the specific case. <strong>When not to apply a form.</strong> When the question does not fit; use the general spine.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-c18-8-roadmap-versus-strategy-goals-frameworks-used-well\"></span>\n<h4>C18.8 Roadmap versus strategy; goals frameworks used well</h4>\n<p>A <strong>roadmap</strong> is a plan of what will be built and when. A strategy is the logic behind it. A <a href=\"#gl-roadmap\">roadmap</a> without a strategy is a list; a strategy without a roadmap is an idea. If the roadmap would be the same under any strategy, it is not an expression of one.</p>\n<p><strong>Goals frameworks.</strong> An <strong>OKR</strong> (objectives and key results) is a goal-setting method pairing a qualitative objective with a small number of measurable key results. The method is widely used <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Doerr, Measure What Matters, 2018: objectives paired with measurable key results]</code> and is often misused. Used well: an objective that states a direction, key results that are outcomes (a measurable change in behavior or results, not a list of tasks), a handful per objective, and an honest review. Misuse: key results that are outputs (&quot;launch feature X&quot;), too many objectives, goals set so safe that they are always met, and scores used as performance ratings, which encourages gaming (C05's Goodhart). Connect goals to the strategy: each objective should serve the guiding policy.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-c18-9-worked-cases\"></span>\n<h4>C18.9 Worked cases</h4>\n<p>Section 5 gives five worked cases: market sizing with a reality check, a market-entry analysis with unit economics, a build-buy-partner decision, a response to a competitor's free tier, and a rewrite of a roadmap into a strategy. Section 11 adds nine questions with model answers, including questions on Apple, Google, Microsoft and Amazon settings.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-c18-10-strategy-theater-how-to-spot-it\"></span>\n<h4>C18.10 Strategy theater: how to spot it</h4>\n<p>Strategy theater is activity that looks like strategy and is not. FW-C18-08 lists the signs (adapted from Rumelt's four hallmarks of bad strategy <code>[V: Rumelt, Good Strategy Bad Strategy (2011): four hallmarks of bad strategy]</code>): <em>fluff</em> (buzzwords such as &quot;synergy,&quot; &quot;best-in-class,&quot; &quot;leverage&quot;); <em>failure to name the challenge</em> (no diagnosis, so any action seems fine); <em>goals mistaken for strategy</em> (&quot;grow 30%&quot;); <em>a list of unrelated initiatives</em>; and <em>no tradeoffs</em> (nothing is declined). The test is simple: can you say what the company will not do, and why?</p>\n<span id=\"c18-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Eight frameworks, set out below. The root-cause toolkit (FW-C03-08), the tradeoff table (FW-C02-03), the decision memo (FW-C02-06), the range method (FW-C04-06), the so-what bridge (FW-C04-05), the low-revenue unit-economics sketch (FW-C09-06), the sizing ladder (FW-C08-06) and the prioritization spine (FW-C15-01) are reused, not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c18-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C18-01 · The strategy kernel · Established</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Origin:</strong> Richard P. Rumelt, <em>Good Strategy/Bad Strategy</em> (2011): diagnosis, guiding policy and coherent action <code>[V: Rumelt, Good Strategy Bad Strategy (2011): the kernel of diagnosis, guiding policy and coherent action]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a situation and an objective.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a diagnosis of the crux, a guiding policy that includes what you will not do, and a set of coherent actions that reinforce one another.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the challenge can be named; choices have costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a focused strategy gives up options.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a goal in place of a diagnosis; a policy that is a wish; actions that do not reinforce.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the rewrite in Worked example 5 and CS-C18-01.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Format: <strong>Diagnosis (the crux) → Guiding policy (the approach, and what we won't do) → Coherent actions (steps that reinforce).</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c18-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C18-02 · The strategy answer spine · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a strategy question and 25 to 30 minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> an answer with seven steps: objective, diagnosis, options, criteria, evaluation with numbers, recommendation and sequence, risks and what would change the view.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the interviewer will answer questions about context.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> structure helps and can feel formulaic; adapt to the case.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a framework with no conclusion; options of one kind; no numbers; no commitment.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the cases in section 5 and the model answers in section 11.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Objective and context → Diagnose → Options (different in kind) → Criteria → Evaluate with numbers → Recommend, sequence → Risks and what would change my mind.</strong> The time budget is in section 4.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c18-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C18-03 · Market sizing with reality checks · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a market definition.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a top-down and a bottom-up estimate, the gap explained, a range, a reality check against known quantities, and the obtainable share.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> order-of-magnitude inputs are enough (C04).</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> two routes cost time and expose errors.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> one route; a percentage of a huge industry; no check; total market treated as obtainable.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the catering example in C18.3 and Worked example 1.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Define → Top-down → Bottom-up → Explain the gap → Range → Reality check → Obtainable share and the so-what.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c18-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C18-04 · The positioning map · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> the alternatives customers consider and what they value.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a two-axis map, each player's position, strengths and what they cannot do, a gap or defensible position, and the likely competitor response.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> axes come from customer research or reasoning about the job.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> two axes simplify; test with a second pair.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> axes that flatter the favorite; feature lists; ignoring substitutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the tiers in Worked example 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c18-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C18-05 · The sources-of-advantage test · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a claimed advantage.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> for each of seven sources (distribution, data, switching costs, network effects, ecosystem integration, scale, brand): present or absent, the mechanism, durability, compounding, and copyability.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the mechanism can be stated in a sentence.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a strict test rejects many claims; that is its value.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> &quot;network effects&quot; as a label; ignoring multi-homing; treating a lead as a moat.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the analysis in CS-C18-02.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>Mechanism</th>\n<th>Durable?</th>\n<th>Compounds?</th>\n<th>Copyable?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c18-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C18-06 · Unit economics at APM depth · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> price, variable costs, frequency, acquisition cost and churn.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> contribution per unit and per month, payback, lifetime contribution, ratio to CAC, two sensitivities and the second-order effects.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> variable costs are separable from fixed.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> early churn is a guess; present ranges.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> gross revenue as margin; allocated fixed cost as variable; one point estimate.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 2.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Formulas: <strong>contribution = revenue − variable cost; payback = CAC ÷ monthly contribution; lifetime = 1 ÷ churn; lifetime contribution = monthly contribution × lifetime.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c18-07\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C18-07 · The build, buy, partner chooser · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a needed capability.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a table comparing the three on time to market, cost, control, strategic importance, capability fit and risk, a multi-year net comparison, and a recommendation with a path (for example, partner now, build later).</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the revenue timeline and costs can be estimated.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> the ranking depends on the horizon and on strategic value not captured in cash.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a single-year view; ignoring integration cost; treating a partner as free.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 3.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Build</th>\n<th>Buy</th>\n<th>Partner</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Time to market</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strategic importance</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c18-08\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C18-08 · The strategy-theater detector · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a strategy document or answer.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a check against five signs (fluff, no named challenge, goals as strategy, a list of unrelated initiatives, no tradeoffs) and a rewrite.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can say what is declined.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> an honest check can be uncomfortable.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> accepting jargon; counting initiatives as coherence.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 5.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sign</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fluff</td>\n<td>Does it say something specific that could be wrong?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No challenge named</td>\n<td>What is the diagnosis?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Goals as strategy</td>\n<td>What is the approach, apart from the target?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unrelated initiatives</td>\n<td>Do the actions reinforce one another?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No tradeoffs</td>\n<td>What will we not do?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c18-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<span id=\"c18-the-strategy-answer-for-a-30-minute-round\"></span>\n<h4>The strategy answer, for a 30-minute round</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Objective and context</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The goal, the company's position, constraints; assumptions stated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diagnose</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Customers and their job; market (size with a reality check); competition and positioning; company capabilities and advantages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Options</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Three or four options that differ in kind</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluate</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Criteria from the objective; unit economics with sensitivities; second-order effects; risks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recommend and sequence</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>The choice, what to do first, the metric, what would change the view</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slack</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules.</strong> If the interviewer gives the objective or the market, spend the saved time on options and evaluation. If you run behind, keep the diagnosis, the recommendation and one number. Treat questions as steering.</p>\n<p><strong>How to say it.</strong> Lead with the crux: &quot;The key question is whether [X].&quot; State the criteria before scoring. State one or two numbers and the assumption that swings them.</p>\n<p><strong>How to close.</strong> Two sentences: &quot;I'd [recommendation], starting with [first step], because [diagnosis and advantage]. The main risk is [risk], and I'd change my view if [signal].&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c18-type-enter-a-market\"></span>\n<h4>Type: enter a market</h4>\n<p>Attractiveness, right to win, economics, risk; then segment and sequence.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-type-respond-to-a-competitor\"></span>\n<h4>Type: respond to a competitor</h4>\n<p>Who is hurt, why they moved, options (ignore, match, differentiate, flank), cost and likely reply.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-type-build-buy-or-partner\"></span>\n<h4>Type: build, buy or partner</h4>\n<p>The chooser (FW-C18-07), over the right horizon, with strategic importance.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-type-should-we-launch-x-bundle-paid-tier-new-line\"></span>\n<h4>Type: should we launch X (bundle, paid tier, new line)?</h4>\n<p>Unit economics, cannibalization, the advantage it builds or erodes, and a test.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-type-prioritize-among-business-lines\"></span>\n<h4>Type: prioritize among business lines</h4>\n<p>Role of each line (fund, grow, experiment), return on goals, protect the cash source.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-type-the-short-version-10-to-15-minutes\"></span>\n<h4>Type: the short version (10 to 15 minutes)</h4>\n<p>Crux, two options, one number, a recommendation and the main risk. Protect the recommendation.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-common-interviewer-interventions-and-what-to-do\"></span>\n<h4>Common interviewer interventions, and what to do</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interviewer says</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What's your point of view?&quot;</td>\n<td>You are listing</td>\n<td>State the crux and a recommendation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How big is it?&quot;</td>\n<td>Market size</td>\n<td>Two routes and a reality check</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Who are the competitors?&quot;</td>\n<td>Beyond the obvious</td>\n<td>Include substitutes and doing nothing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Why would customers stay?&quot;</td>\n<td>Moat</td>\n<td>Name the mechanism and test durability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Does it make money?&quot;</td>\n<td>Economics</td>\n<td>Give contribution, payback and a sensitivity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What will they do in response?&quot;</td>\n<td>Competitor reaction</td>\n<td>Say what they can and cannot do quickly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What won't you do?&quot;</td>\n<td>Strategy as choice</td>\n<td>Name what you decline and why</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c18-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All five examples are <code>Illustrative</code>: the products are fictional or use a product type as the setting only, and every figure is an invented assumption. The arithmetic was computed. Each follows the strategy spine (FW-C18-02) and is written as notes you could say aloud.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-worked-example-1-sizing-a-market-with-two-routes-and-a-reali\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — Sizing a market with two routes and a reality check (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The prompt.</strong> &quot;Size the market for a pet-health telemedicine subscription in a country of 25 million households.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Top-down (FW-C18-03).</strong> 25,000,000 households; 40% own a pet, so 10,000,000. Suppose 5% would pay for a telemedicine subscription: 500,000 households, at $120 a year: $60 million a year (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Bottom-up.</strong> Count the places that could sell it. 12,000 veterinary clinics; suppose 20% would offer a telemedicine package; each averages 300 subscribers at $120: 12,000 × 0.20 × 300 × $120 = $86.4 million a year.</p>\n<p><strong>The gap.</strong> The bottom-up figure is 1.4 times the top-down. Both rely on an adoption guess (5% of owners; 20% of clinics with 300 subscribers). The two are in the same range, which is mildly reassuring, though they share optimistic assumptions about adoption.</p>\n<p><strong>Reality check.</strong> A known provider in the category (suppose a public company reports 40,000 subscribers at $120, invented) has revenue of about $4.8 million. If the market were $60 million, the leader would have 8% of it, plausible in an early market. It also shows the current realized market is a small fraction of the potential: the $60 million is a three-to-five-year serviceable ceiling (if adoption grows), not today's market. Another check: if households spend $250 a year on veterinary visits per pet and telemedicine can replace some, the plausible shift is a small share of that spend, supporting a figure in the tens of millions, not hundreds.</p>\n<p><strong>The range and the so-what.</strong> A planning range of $40 million to $60 million a year in three to five years. An entrant that wins 10% earns $4 to $6 million a year in revenue. Is that enough? It depends on the cost of serving (veterinarian time) and acquisition. So the sizing sets up the next question: unit economics and the channel (clinics as partners reduce acquisition cost). I'd also state the swing assumption: adoption of 5% could be 2% or 10%, a fivefold range, so the first goal is evidence on adoption (a pilot with a few clinics).</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Two routes exposed shared optimism, the reality check separated potential from today, and the number was tied to a decision.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-worked-example-2-market-entry-density-decides-the-economics-\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — Market entry: density decides the economics (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a retail marketplace of the kind Amazon operates, considering grocery delivery in a new city. No fact about any real company is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The question.</strong> Should the marketplace enter grocery delivery in a new city, and where first?</p>\n<p><strong>Attractive and winnable.</strong> Grocery is frequent (weekly), large, and a customer who orders groceries uses the platform more often. The right to win rests on an existing customer base, logistics and payments. The market is contested by local grocers and delivery services.</p>\n<p><strong>Unit economics (FW-C18-06).</strong> Average order $80; gross margin 22% ($17.60); picking and delivery cost depends on density. Suppose a delivery route costs $30 per route-hour (driver, vehicle, handling) (invented). Deliveries per route-hour determine cost per order:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Neighborhood</th>\n<th>Drops per route-hour</th>\n<th>Delivery cost per order</th>\n<th>Contribution per order</th>\n<th>Per month (3 orders)</th>\n<th>Lifetime (12.5 months)</th>\n<th>Ratio to CAC of $40</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sparse suburb</td>\n<td>2.5</td>\n<td>$12.00</td>\n<td>$5.60</td>\n<td>$16.80</td>\n<td>$210</td>\n<td>5.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mid-density</td>\n<td>4.0</td>\n<td>$7.50</td>\n<td>$10.10</td>\n<td>$30.30</td>\n<td>$379</td>\n<td>9.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dense city core</td>\n<td>6.0</td>\n<td>$5.00</td>\n<td>$12.60</td>\n<td>$37.80</td>\n<td>$473</td>\n<td>11.8</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Churn of 8% a month gives the 12.5-month lifetime. The break-even density is where contribution is zero: $30 ÷ $17.60 = 1.7 drops per route-hour. Below about two drops an hour each order loses money; at four or more it earns well.</p>\n<p><strong>The sensitivity.</strong> Basket size and density are the swing factors. A basket of $60 cuts gross margin to $13.20; in the sparse suburb contribution becomes $1.20 (the lifetime ratio falls to about 1.1), in the dense core $8.20. So the entry sequence is: start in the densest neighborhoods, and enter the sparse ones only when density or basket size has grown.</p>\n<p><strong>Risks and second-order effects.</strong> Local competitors may respond with discounts; supply (picking staff, vehicles) must be hired before demand; perishable goods raise waste. Customers acquired for groceries may buy more of other categories (an upside not counted).</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> Enter with a pilot in two dense neighborhoods, with explicit thresholds: at least 4 drops per route-hour and a basket above $70 within six months, a 90-day retention target, and a stop rule if contribution per order is not positive after six months. Expand by adding adjacent neighborhoods to raise density. What would change my view: a competitor with much lower delivery cost, or a basket size far below $60.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The strategic question reduced to one driver (density), which pointed to where to start and how to tell if it was working.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-worked-example-3-build-buy-or-partner-illustrative\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — Build, buy or partner (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: an enterprise software suite of the kind Microsoft offers. No fact about any real company is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The question.</strong> The suite lacks a compliance-archiving capability that large customers ask for. Build, buy or partner?</p>\n<p><strong>Facts (invented).</strong> At full run-rate the capability would add $20 million a year of revenue at a 70% contribution margin. <em>Build:</em> 12 months to ship, $6 million to build plus $1 million a year to maintain. <em>Buy:</em> acquire a startup for $45 million; 6 months to integrate; the team stays. <em>Partner:</em> integrate a vendor's product in 3 months; the vendor takes 25% of the revenue (so contribution falls to 45% of revenue).</p>\n<p><strong>Criteria (FW-C18-07).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Build</th>\n<th>Buy</th>\n<th>Partner</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Time to market</td>\n<td>12 months</td>\n<td>6 months</td>\n<td>3 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost</td>\n<td>$6M + $1M a year</td>\n<td>$45M</td>\n<td>25% of revenue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control of roadmap</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strategic importance</td>\n<td>Core for large customers</td>\n<td>Same</td>\n<td>Dependency on a vendor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main risk</td>\n<td>Execution, time</td>\n<td>Integration, price paid</td>\n<td>Vendor leverage, margin</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Net over time.</strong> Revenue starts when the capability ships and is assumed to run at $20 million a year (a simplification). <em>Three years:</em> build earns 24 months of revenue ($40M) × 0.70 = $28M, less $6M and two years of maintenance ($2M), net $20M. Buy: 30 months ($50M) × 0.70 = $35M, less $45M, net −$10M. Partner: 33 months ($55M) × 0.45 = $24.75M. At three years, partner leads ($24.75M), build is close ($20M), buy is negative.</p>\n<p><em>Five years:</em> build: 48 months ($80M) × 0.70 = $56M less $6M and four years of maintenance ($4M) = $46M. Buy: 54 months ($90M) × 0.70 = $63M less $45M = $18M. Partner: 57 months ($95M) × 0.45 = $42.75M. At five years, build leads ($46M), partner is close ($42.75M), buy $18M.</p>\n<p><strong>Reading it.</strong> The answer depends on the horizon. Partner wins on speed and low upfront cost, and loses margin every year; build wins if the capability is core and the horizon is long. Buying makes sense only if the acquired team and technology are worth more than the numbers above show (other products, talent, a faster path in other areas), which would need its own case.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> Partner now to reach customers in three months, and start building in parallel with a plan to replace the vendor in 12 to 18 months, if the capability proves to be core (large customers adopt). This captures early revenue and keeps the long-run margin, at the cost of running two efforts for a time and of vendor negotiation (a contract that allows the transition). What would change my view: a startup whose team and data we could not build ourselves (a case for buying), or a vendor who would not agree to a transition.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A small multi-year calculation turned a debate into a horizon question, and the recommendation combined options.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-worked-example-4-a-competitor-launches-a-free-tier-illustrat\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — A competitor launches a free tier (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A subscription product has 100,000 paying members at $10 a month. A competitor launches a free tier with ads. The team asks whether to match.</p>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis.</strong> Who is at risk? The competitor's free tier mainly attracts price-sensitive and casual users; our heavy users value features it lacks. The crux: how many of our paying members would move to a free tier, and whether a free tier of our own would bring in enough new payers and ad revenue to offset.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> (A) Ignore. (B) Match with a free tier. (C) Differentiate: keep the paid product, emphasize what the free tier lacks, and improve value for paying members. (D) Flank: a lower-priced tier for casual users, protecting the main product.</p>\n<p><strong>Economics of matching (B), per month (invented).</strong> Suppose a free tier draws 300,000 free users, 4% of whom convert to paid at $10 (12,000 payers, $120,000), and the ads earn $0.50 per free user ($150,000). A share of current payers would downgrade to free. If 15% downgrade: −100,000 × 0.15 × $10 = −$150,000. Net: −$150,000 + $120,000 + $150,000 = +$120,000 a month. If 25% downgrade: −$250,000 + $270,000 = +$20,000. If conversion is only 2% (6,000 payers, $60,000) and 15% downgrade: −$150,000 + $60,000 + $150,000 = +$60,000. The break-even downgrade rate (with 4% conversion): ($120,000 + $150,000) ÷ $1,000,000 = 27%. If more than about 27% of payers would downgrade, matching loses money.</p>\n<p><strong>What we don't know, and how to learn it.</strong> The downgrade rate is the swing assumption. Our payers' features and engagement tell us who would leave: members who use only the basic features are most at risk. A survey is weak evidence (the say-do gap); a test of a free tier in one market is better.</p>\n<p><strong>Competitor response.</strong> The competitor can cut price or add features quickly; it cannot easily remove the free tier without losing the users it won. A free tier also changes positioning: we become the paid alternative to free, or we become a freemium product.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> Do not match in full. Choose D: a limited, lower-priced tier that is hard to downgrade to from our heavy-use features (it lacks them), and a differentiation push for the main product. Test the lower tier in one market for a quarter, and measure downgrades, conversions and ad revenue, with a stop rule if downgrades pass 20%. What would change my view: evidence that a majority of payers use only features the free tier offers.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A break-even downgrade rate (27%) turned &quot;should we match?&quot; into a measurable question, and a narrower response protected the core.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-worked-example-5-a-roadmap-dressed-as-a-strategy-illustrativ\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 5 — A roadmap dressed as a strategy (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>Before.</strong> A team's &quot;strategy&quot; slide for a clinic-scheduling tool (invented): <em>Grow revenue 40%. Launch a mobile app. Add analytics. Build 10 integrations. Add an AI assistant. Raise NPS to 50. Enter two new markets.</em> This is a list of goals and projects: fluff-free but without a diagnosis, a choice or a tradeoff (FW-C18-08).</p>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis.</strong> From the data (invented): 70% of revenue comes from small clinics (one to three providers). 60% of churn happens in the first 90 days, and exit interviews cite setup effort (&quot;too much to configure&quot;). Large clinics ask for integrations with their records systems, which the product cannot yet support, and are served worse than by competitors. The crux: the product wins where setup is simple and loses where complexity rises.</p>\n<p><strong>Guiding policy (FW-C18-01).</strong> Win small clinics by being the easiest scheduling tool to set up and keep. Decline custom integrations for large clinics and new markets this year.</p>\n<p><strong>Coherent actions.</strong> (1) Guided setup that gets a clinic scheduling its first appointment within 30 minutes. (2) An import tool for existing calendars. (3) A referral loop through the associations that small clinics already belong to. (4) Simplified pricing (one plan). (5) Retention review at day 90. Each action reinforces the policy: easier setup lowers early churn, referral lowers acquisition cost, and simpler pricing removes a barrier.</p>\n<p><strong>What is declined and why.</strong> Integrations (cost and skill), the AI assistant (not tied to the crux), mobile (secondary for a front-desk tool), new markets (focus). The reasons are stated, with a date to revisit.</p>\n<p><strong>Targets that follow from the strategy.</strong> 90-day retention from 62% to 75%; time to first appointment under 30 minutes for 80% of new clinics; referral share of new sign-ups from 10% to 30% (invented). Revenue growth follows from these, instead of being the strategy.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The same company, rewritten as a diagnosis, a policy and reinforcing actions, now says what it will not do, and its goals measure the policy.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;A competitor just launched a free version of a product like ours. How do you respond?&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c18-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd do a SWOT and look at the five forces. We should probably lower our price or add features to stay competitive, and invest in marketing.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> frameworks named, no diagnosis, no options that differ in kind, and a response that is a guess. No numbers, no commitment.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;First I'd diagnose: who is at risk? A free version mostly attracts price-sensitive and casual users. I'd segment our customers by how much they use features the free version lacks, and estimate how many might switch.</p>\n<p>&quot;Options: ignore it, match it with a free tier, differentiate the paid product, or flank with a cheaper tier for casual users. I'd evaluate them on cost, effect on our economics and the competitor's likely reply. The key number is the share of our payers who would downgrade, against the new payers and ad revenue a free tier would bring.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd likely recommend protecting the segment at risk with a narrower response than matching, and testing it in one market before committing.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c18-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd put a number on the break-even: if a free tier would convert 4% of free users and earn some ad revenue, matching pays only if fewer than about a quarter of our payers downgrade, so the downgrade rate is the assumption to test. I'd check cannibalization in both directions and the effect on positioning: matching risks turning us into a freemium product competing on the competitor's terms.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd ask what the competitor can and cannot do. They can cut price quickly; they cannot remove the free tier without losing what they won. So I'd choose a response that makes our advantage clearer rather than imitating their move: the value of features and service that the free version lacks.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd name what we will not do (a price war), what would change my view (evidence that most payers use only free-tier features), and the first step (a one-market test with a stop rule). And I'd separate the diagnosis from the action so that if the data show the threat is smaller, we spend less.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c18-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Diagnoses who is at risk and offers options that differ in kind</td>\n<td>Computes a break-even for the key assumption and tests it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluates on cost, economics and reply</td>\n<td>Considers positioning and what the competitor cannot do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recommends a narrower response and a test</td>\n<td>Names what is declined, what would change the view and a stop rule</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c18-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge. Interview formats change <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple describes integrating hardware, software and services and reports services as a business line <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Apple Form 10-K for fiscal 2025: Services net sales 109.2 billion dollars]</code>. Third-party guides report that strategy prompts at Apple concern ecosystem decisions such as bundling services or responding to platform regulation <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: treat ecosystem integration as the advantage to test, weigh cannibalization of existing subscriptions, and discuss second-order effects on security, quality and developers; label regulatory facts as to be verified.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Google's free, advertising-funded consumer products and its cloud business are public <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Alphabet Form 10-K for 2025]</code>. Candidates commonly report strategy prompts about monetizing a free product or responding to new technologies <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: compute what a paid tier does to ad revenue, consider selection (who would pay), and say how a response affects the core business.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon has described a flywheel of selection, low prices and customer experience, and its shareholder letters emphasize long-term thinking <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon flywheel as described in AWS material and Bezos letters]</code>; its leadership principles include Think Big and Customer Obsession <code>Public</code>. Candidates commonly report market-entry and marketplace-conflict prompts <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: explain the flywheel mechanism, include unit economics and density, and address trust with third parties where the platform also competes.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft's 2014 statement of a mobile-first, cloud-first direction is public <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Nadella email to all employees, 10 July 2014: productivity and platform company for the mobile-first and cloud-first world]</code>, and its enterprise customer base is public in its annual reports (not reopened here) <code>Inferred</code>. Candidates commonly report build, buy or partner and portfolio-allocation prompts for enterprise settings <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: separate the roles of mature, growing and experimental lines, and show how a platform and its partners create value together.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Meta's reliance on advertising is public <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Meta Form 10-K for 2025 (advertising)]</code>, and its acquisitions are widely known (not rechecked here) <code>Inferred</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts on competing with a fast-growing rival, acquisition, and product positioning for different audiences <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: discuss network effects precisely (who benefits from whom), multi-homing and the response to a rival that copies.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix's move from DVDs by mail to streaming and then to original content, and the later addition of an advertising tier, are public <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix streaming launch 2007; ad tier launched 3 November 2022 (Fast Company, Netflix IR as reported)]</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts on pricing, content strategy and competition <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: connect content spending to retention and unit economics, and treat price tiers as segmentation.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Stripe's expansion from payments into a wider set of financial products for businesses is public <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Stripe newsroom: Connect, Billing, Terminal, Radar, Capital, Corporate Card]</code>. Candidates report prompts on platform expansion and developer-product strategy <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: explain how the first product creates distribution and data for the next, and how switching costs arise from integration.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c18-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>A framework with no conclusion</td>\n<td>SWOT recited, no recommendation</td>\n<td>Diagnose, choose, commit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Goals as strategy</td>\n<td>&quot;Grow 40%&quot;</td>\n<td>State the approach and what is declined</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>One-route market size</td>\n<td>A percentage of a huge industry</td>\n<td>Two routes and a reality check</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Feature table as competitive analysis</td>\n<td>A grid of checkmarks</td>\n<td>Position by customer value; consider substitutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>A moat claimed by label</td>\n<td>&quot;Network effects&quot; with no mechanism</td>\n<td>Name the mechanism; test durability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>No unit economics</td>\n<td>Growth without margin</td>\n<td>Contribution, payback, ratio, two sensitivities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Ignoring cannibalization</td>\n<td>Gross new revenue counted</td>\n<td>Net it against what it replaces</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Ignoring competitor response</td>\n<td>A move that assumes no reply</td>\n<td>Say what they can and cannot do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>A single-horizon comparison</td>\n<td>Build versus partner at one year</td>\n<td>Compare over the relevant horizon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>A roadmap presented as strategy</td>\n<td>A list of projects</td>\n<td>Rewrite as diagnosis, policy, actions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c18-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter. <a href=\"#gl-survivorship-bias\">survivorship bias</a> is from C12 and <a href=\"#gl-opportunity-cost\">opportunity cost</a> from C15.</p>\n<p><strong>Where you play, how you win</strong> (<code>MM-play-win</code>). Strategy reduces to two choices: which customers and markets you serve, and the basis on which you beat the alternatives. Writing them in one sentence each is a test of clarity. If the &quot;how to win&quot; would apply to any competitor, it is not a choice.</p>\n<p><strong>The flywheel</strong> (<code>MM-flywheel</code>). A reinforcing loop in which each element feeds the next: more selection attracts more customers, more customers attract more sellers, more sellers add selection. Draw the loop and ask where it starts, what slows it and whether a competitor can enter it at a point of weakness. A flywheel is a way to explain a network effect or scale advantage concretely.</p>\n<p><strong>Second-order effects</strong> (<code>MM-second-order</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-second-order-effect\">second-order effect</a>, C02). The first effect of a move is visible; the effects of the responses to it are where strategy succeeds or fails: competitors react, partners change terms, regulators notice, customers adapt. Ask &quot;and then what?&quot; twice.</p>\n<p><strong>Survivorship in strategy stories</strong> (<code>MM-survivorship</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-survivorship-bias\">survivorship bias</a>, C12). The strategies that are famous are those that worked. Learn from a success by asking which conditions were specific to the place and time, and look for failures that followed the same approach.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineering.</strong> Engineers can say what is easy, hard and time-consuming to build, which drives build, buy or partner. They also know the architecture's constraints on ecosystem integration. Involve them in sizing the cost and the time to market.</li>\n<li><strong>Design and research.</strong> Research tells you what customers actually value, which is the basis for positioning axes and the answer to &quot;why would they switch?&quot; Use it to test positioning claims.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> Data scientists help test the assumptions that swing a strategy: churn, downgrade rates, conversion, willingness to pay. Ask for ranges and for cheap experiments.</li>\n<li><strong>Finance, legal and policy.</strong> Finance supplies cost structure and the discipline of unit economics; legal and policy teams identify regulatory constraints that can reshape options (competition rules, data rules). Involve them early in any move that affects partners or markets <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c18-checklist-before-you-recommend-a-strategy\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: before you recommend a strategy</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I state the objective and diagnose the situation before proposing anything?</li>\n<li>Did I size the opportunity with two routes and a reality check?</li>\n<li>Did I analyze competition by customer value, including substitutes and likely responses?</li>\n<li>Did I name the source of advantage and test its durability?</li>\n<li>Did I run unit economics with two sensitivities and net out cannibalization?</li>\n<li>Are my options different in kind, and compared over the right horizon?</li>\n<li>Did I commit to a recommendation, say what is declined and sequence the first steps?</li>\n<li>Did I say what would change my view, and avoid roadmap-as-strategy and framework theater?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c18-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c18-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C18-01 · The coffee chain's expansion · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented (invented).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> A coffee-cart chain has 10 carts. Each earns $1,000 a day in revenue with a 28% contribution margin ($280). Management wants to open more carts, reasoning that a new cart at $900 a day brings $252 a day of contribution and pays back its $30,000 cost in 119 days.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Capital for a few carts a year, a limited number of good locations, and managers who can supervise only so many sites.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Sales data show that when a new cart opens within 400 meters of an existing one, the existing cart's sales fall by about 20%. Most proposed new locations are within 400 meters of one or two existing carts.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> As an observable problem: &quot;The expansion case counts the new cart's revenue as additional, though part of it comes from the chain's own carts.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> Net the cannibalization. A new cart near one existing cart adds $252 a day of contribution and removes 20% of that cart's $280, $56, for a net of $196 a day: payback is $30,000 ÷ $196 = 153 days, not 119. Near two existing carts it removes $112, for a net of $140 a day and a payback of 214 days. In a location 500 meters or more from any cart, the net is the full $252 and the payback 119 days. So the same expansion budget buys more profit in uncannibalized locations, and the case for an infill location depends on whether it is worth 214 days of payback. The strategic reading: the chain's advantage in a neighborhood is local density of customers who walk by, and adding carts beyond a point shares the same customers.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Open carts wherever space is available</td>\n<td>Number of carts</td>\n<td>Cannibalization; slower payback</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Prioritize locations more than 500 meters from existing carts</td>\n<td>Net contribution per dollar</td>\n<td>Fewer available sites; possibly lower foot traffic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Improve the existing carts (hours, menu, speed)</td>\n<td>Contribution per cart</td>\n<td>Does not add reach</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B gives the best payback if good non-overlapping sites exist. C is cheap and limited by the carts' capacity. A inflates the count and the cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Choose B, with C for the carts that have idle time. Rule: open a new cart only if net payback (after cannibalization) is under 180 days. Primary metric: net contribution per dollar invested; guardrail: the sales of the nearest existing carts after each opening.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario three new carts were opened at least 500 meters from existing ones and averaged $240 a day of net contribution; one infill location was declined (payback 214 days). Sales at neighboring carts were tracked and fell as predicted at a second infill site, near one cart (payback 153 days), opened as a pilot (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If foot traffic at remote sites were much lower, the net would fall below the infill case, and B would be wrong; the pilot would show it. The lesson: count the net, not the gross, and know what advantage the business actually has in a place.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c18-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C18-02 · Amazon Web Services, 2006: turning internal infrastructure into a product · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): Amazon Web Services' own posts and announcements: Jeff Barr's blog posts of 14 March 2006 (S3) and 25 August 2006 (the EC2 beta), the S3 &quot;What's New&quot; announcement of 14 March 2006 as quoted in the AWS News Blog, and the AWS News Blog's twenty-year retrospective of 13 March 2026, read in full (primary, for dates, prices and design intent); Amazon's 2015 annual report on Form 10-K and press reports of its first-quarter 2015 results (for the segment disclosure); and reference timelines for the later EC2 dates, marked as secondary. The analysis is the author's. The sources do not reveal internal deliberations or strategy documents. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> On 14 March 2006 Amazon launched the Simple Storage Service (S3), described in AWS's retrospective as quietly launched with a one-paragraph announcement, offering storage over the internet at 15 cents per gigabyte per month, with data transfer at 20 cents per gigabyte <code>[V: Jeff Barr, 14 March 2006]</code>, on about a petabyte of capacity across roughly 400 storage nodes in 15 racks spanning three data centers, with 15 Gbps of total bandwidth, a design target of tens of billions of objects and a maximum object size of 5 gigabytes <code>[V: AWS News Blog, 13 March 2026]</code>. On 25 August 2006 it opened the Elastic Compute Cloud (EC2) for limited beta testing, offering a virtual computer (the equivalent of a 1.7 GHz Xeon processor with 1.75 GB of RAM) built on the Xen virtual machine monitor <code>[V: Jeff Barr, 25 August 2006]</code>; press and reference timelines report a price of 10 cents an hour, access at first only to existing AWS customers, an unlimited public beta from 16 October 2007 (Amazon's press release of that date says the beta, which had limited the number of new registrants, was now open to all developers; some reference timelines give 22 August 2007, which conflicts with that announcement) and the end of the beta on 23 October 2008 <code>[V: Amazon Web Services press release, 16 October 2007; secondary for the price and the end of the beta]</code>. AWS's launch announcement presented S3 as giving any developer access to the same scalable, reliable, fast and inexpensive storage infrastructure that Amazon used to run its own network of web sites <code>[V: AWS announcement as quoted in the AWS News Blog, 2026]</code>. The sources used here do not describe Amazon's internal reasoning before the launch.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Amazon was an online retailer with no history of selling computing to others <code>[V: AWS launch announcement]</code>; selling to outside developers would have required things a retailer's internal systems did not need, such as identity, authentication, billing and security for external customers <code>[inference]</code>; the sources read here do not describe how Amazon built them. Its customers would be developers, who choose tools on merit and switch easily at first. The offering had to be priced for use, not for upfront commitment.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As reported by AWS: the launch dates, prices and capacity above; AWS's statement in 2026 that code written for S3 in 2006 still works unchanged, with complete API backward compatibility described as its commitment <code>[V: AWS News Blog, 2026]</code>; a fall in S3's price from 15 cents to slightly over 2 cents per gigabyte, about 85%, over twenty years; and growth from about a petabyte to hundreds of exabytes. Amazon has reported AWS as a separate segment since the first quarter of 2015; its first disclosure, on 23 April 2015, showed AWS net sales of $1.57 billion and operating income of $265 million for the quarter <code>[V: Amazon 2015 Form 10-K; press reports of the 23 April 2015 results]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>The author's reading.</em> Developers and companies faced large upfront costs and effort to buy and run servers and storage before they knew whether a product would succeed. Amazon had solved the same problem for itself. The strategic question: sell the capability to others, and on what terms?</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's analysis.</em> Through this chapter's tools:</p>\n<p><em>Diagnosis and policy (FW-C18-01).</em> The crux was that infrastructure was a large fixed cost and a distraction for the people building products. The guiding policy, as the launches suggest, was to offer simple, well-specified building blocks (storage, then compute) over the internet, priced by use, with programming interfaces that developers could adopt without a sales process. This is a choice and a set of declined options: a single abstract service with a stable interface, not custom contracts.</p>\n<p><em>Positioning (C18.4).</em> The alternatives were buying servers, renting colocation space, or using managed hosting. AWS's position was on speed of start, no upfront commitment, and elasticity, trading away the customization of owning hardware.</p>\n<p><em>Sources of advantage (FW-C18-05).</em> <em>Scale:</em> Amazon already ran large infrastructure for its retail business, and falling costs could be passed to customers: AWS reports an 85% fall in S3's price since launch <code>[V: AWS News Blog, 2026]</code>, and attributing it to scale and learning is the author's inference, since the post does not give a cause <code>[inference]</code>. <em>Switching costs:</em> once a customer's data and code are built on a service's interfaces, moving is costly; the promise that old code keeps working is the other side of this, a trust signal that lowers the fear of lock-in while raising the reward of staying. <em>Ecosystem:</em> each new service (databases, networking, identity) works with the others, so customers' investment compounds. <em>Distribution:</em> developers adopt through documentation and self-service, a low-cost channel. There is no strong network effect in the sense of C18.5, and it would be wrong to claim one; the advantage is scale, integration and switching costs.</p>\n<p><em>Unit economics (FW-C18-06).</em> A usage-based price converts a fixed cost for customers into a variable one, and for the provider turns fixed infrastructure into revenue from many customers, with utilization as the swing factor. The sources do not disclose margins.</p>\n<p><em>Sequence.</em> Storage first (a simple, universal need), compute months later, then many more services: a pattern of landing a clear building block and extending.</p>\n<p><em>What the evidence does not show.</em> Whether the original intent was to build a large business or to monetize spare capacity; AWS's profitability in the early years; and how much of the later success owes to timing, competitor mistakes or execution. The case supports an analysis of the structure of the advantage, not a claim about intentions.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction of choices a team could have faced; not a record of Amazon's deliberations.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Keep the infrastructure internal as an efficiency tool</td>\n<td>Focus on retail</td>\n<td>Forgoes a new business; others may own the category</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Sell simple, usage-priced building blocks with self-service interfaces</td>\n<td>Reach among developers; scale; learning</td>\n<td>New capabilities (billing, support, security for outsiders); commitment to stable interfaces</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Sell through traditional enterprise contracts and sales teams</td>\n<td>Large accounts</td>\n<td>Slow start; cost of sales; less developer adoption</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B gives up the high-touch margins of enterprise sales for speed and scale, and takes on the obligation of stability. A is low risk and low reward. C fits an established vendor.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, Amazon launched S3 and then EC2 on the terms above.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Reported: continued expansion of services; price reductions; the backward compatibility that AWS describes as its commitment, upheld according to AWS itself; AWS reported as a separate segment from 2015 <code>[V: Amazon 2015 Form 10-K]</code>. Beyond the first-quarter 2015 figures, the sources used here do not give financial results.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> Had the services required enterprise sales cycles, adoption by start-ups and developers, the early customers, would have been slower. Had early pricing been set higher, competitors might have entered the low end. A reader should resist reading the outcome as proof that the decision was obviously right at the time: the same facts would be discussed differently if a competitor had won the category. The method to carry away: describe the advantage by its mechanism, check it for durability, tie the business model to the customer's cost structure, and sequence the offering from a clear building block.</p>\n<span id=\"c18-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Nine questions: two Beginner, two Intermediate, five Hard. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code> (composite candidates), every figure is an assumption, and the arithmetic was computed. Each is written against the six behaviors in section 1 and uses the strategy spine (FW-C18-02). Where a real company's product or a regulation is the setting, the answer reasons from public information and states no internal fact; regulatory facts are marked for verification. The answers are fuller than a first pass in a real round.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c18-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C18-01 · Beginner · strategy · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What is strategy, and how is it different from a plan or a goal?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's the long-term plan to achieve goals.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines strategy as choices based on a diagnosis, distinct from goals and plans.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Gives the kernel (diagnosis, guiding policy, coherent actions), includes what is declined, and offers a short example and a test for bad strategy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> A goal is where you want to end up. A plan is a list of steps and dates. A strategy is the logic that connects them: a diagnosis of the situation, an approach chosen to deal with it, and actions that reinforce each other. The distinguishing feature is choice, and especially what you decide not to do.</p>\n<p>An example. A clinic-scheduling product says: &quot;Grow 40% and launch mobile, analytics, integrations and an AI assistant.&quot; That is a goal and a project list. A strategy would read: &quot;Diagnosis: 70% of our revenue comes from small clinics, who churn in the first 90 days because setup is hard, while large clinics need integrations we cannot deliver. Policy: win small clinics by being the easiest to set up; decline large-clinic integrations and new markets this year. Actions: guided setup, a calendar import tool, referral through clinic associations, one simple price.&quot; The actions all push in the same direction, and the declined items are named.</p>\n<p>A quick test for a real strategy: can you say what the company will not do, and why? If every initiative fits, it is a wish list. Another test: would the plan look the same under any strategy? Then it expresses none.</p>\n<p>At my level, strategy shows up as understanding the company's choices and connecting my product's decisions to them, and as the ability to reason about a strategic question when it arises.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n";
